00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:32.112
<v NARRATOR>The fire had burned down to the good part, the orange-glowing part, and the circle of kids leaned in close, because Toss and Latch had promised them one story told by two voices.

4
00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:37.083
<v TOSS>Once — there was a fox who owed the moon a favor.

5
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:59.534
<v NARRATOR>Toss always went first; she loved the launch of a tale, throwing the first line up into the dark like a spark. She let the fox creep three sentences into the woods, building the night around it — and then she tossed the story to Latch, turning to him with her eyebrows up, handing him the thread mid-air.

6
00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:04.905
<v LATCH>Uh — and the fox, um, walked to a river.

7
00:01:04.905 --> 00:01:20.756
<v NARRATOR>He caught it. Sort of. His voice came out nothing like the hushed, curling tone Toss had set. The story hit the ground with a thud you could almost hear. The spell wobbled.

8
00:01:20.756 --> 00:01:27.167
<v LATCH>I keep dropping it. The second you toss it to me, I can't remember how you were saying it.

9
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:43.658
<v NARRATOR>The trouble wasn't that Latch couldn't tell a story — alone, he was wonderful, all rumble and patience. The trouble was the seam. Toss would hand him a slow, spooky moment and he'd catch it at a gallop.

10
00:01:43.658 --> 00:01:53.309
<v TOSS>I've been so busy loving my own launches that I never once thought about how I was landing the story into your hands.

11
00:01:53.309 --> 00:01:59.080
<v NARRATOR>So they stopped practicing the words. They practiced the handoff.

12
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:08.371
<v TOSS>I learned to hand the thread over at the END of a breath, not the middle of one — to leave Latch a clean edge to grab.

13
00:02:08.371 --> 00:02:27.782
<v LATCH>And I learned the real secret nobody had told me: before I open my mouth, I catch the last note the other teller left in the air. If Toss ends low and slow, I start low and slow — matching her the way you match a friend's walking pace without thinking.

14
00:02:27.782 --> 00:02:41.833
<v NARRATOR>They tried the fox again. Toss ended a hushed line low and soft — and Latch caught it low and soft, and carried the fox on through the moonlit woods without a bump.

15
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:46.324
<v TOSS>There. Did you feel that? No seam.

16
00:02:46.324 --> 00:02:57.655
<v NARRATOR>And the circle couldn't tell, anymore, where one voice ended and the other began. Two tellers had become one telling.

17
00:02:57.655 --> 00:03:06.826
<v LATCH>Listen for HOW they're telling it, not just when to jump in. Catch the note. Match it. Carry it on.

18
00:03:06.826 --> 00:03:19.397
<v NARRATOR>And the fox padded on toward the moon, passed hand to hand so smoothly that the kids forgot there were two tellers at all — which was, of course, the whole point.

